## Scope

The entire repository is AGPL-3.0-only — every directory and every language,
without exception. There is no dual licence, no per-directory rule and no
commercial-use carve-out.

Copyleft here is a choice rather than an inheritance. The Python scientific
stack the engine builds on is uniformly permissive — BSD-3-Clause, Apache-2.0
and MIT — so no dependency compels any licence at all. The Affero variant is
chosen because the deployed form of this project is a hosted compute engine, and
section 13 is what obliges an operator who modifies it to offer the
corresponding source to the users who reach it over a network. `LICENSE` sets
out that reasoning in full before the licence text.

One constraint follows and is recorded here so that it is not rediscovered
during a dependency review: AGPL-3.0 is **not** compatible with GPL-2.0-only
code, so a GPL-2.0-only dependency can no longer be admitted to this tree.


## Third-party components

The runtime image ships pinned third-party Python packages. Their attribution
register — every package, version and declared licence — is in
`engine/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md`, generated from the committed `uv.lock` by
`engine/scripts/gen_third_party.py` and gated against drift in continuous
integration. A machine-readable bill of materials is in `engine/sbom.cdx.json`
(CycloneDX).

Each installed distribution keeps its own metadata and, where it ships one, its
own licence file, inside the image under the virtual environment's
`site-packages`.

CPython itself is distributed under the Python Software Foundation License,
which is permissive and GPL-compatible; its licence text ships with the
interpreter.

Galaxy is MIT-licensed and is an upstream dependency, not vendored code. A
permissive dependency combines with a copyleft work without difficulty.


## Corresponding source

Corresponding source for the copyleft components is available three ways:

1. **From the upstream index** — every package is pinned in `uv.lock` by exact
   name, version and hash. PyPI artifacts are immutable: a released filename and
   its hash can never be replaced, so the pin identifies exact bytes. Sources are
   reachable per package at `https://pypi.org/project/<NAME>/<VERSION>/`.

2. **By reproducing the build** — `uv sync --locked` against the committed
   `uv.lock` retrieves the identical artifacts on any machine, and verifies each
   one against the recorded hash.

3. **On request** — the Licensor will provide, for any recipient of the image
   and for at least three years, a complete machine-readable copy of the
   corresponding source of the copyleft components, on a medium customarily used
   for software interchange, for no more than the cost of distribution.

   Contact: open an issue titled "AGPL source request" at
   <https://github.com/Deadalus-Lab/EconFlow-Analytics/issues>. If the repository is
   ever unavailable, use GitHub's private vulnerability reporting on the same
   repository, which reaches the maintainers directly.

   This is a channel rather than an email address, deliberately: a published
   address is permanently harvested, and git authorship already uses GitHub's
   routed no-reply address for the same reason. The obligation is unaffected —
   the channel is monitored, and a request made through it is answered on the
   terms stated above.


## Network use

Section 13 is the operative clause for anyone running this software as a
service. If you modify EconFlow Analytics and let users interact with it
remotely over a network, you must offer those users the corresponding source of
your modified version. Deploying without distributing a binary does not avoid
the obligation; that is the entire point of choosing the Affero variant.

The engine's own source — the 251 wrappers, the gates and the tests — is in this
repository, licensed AGPL-3.0-only. See `LICENSE`.
